# View Conventions

## Views Are HTML with Sprinkles

Views render the model for the browser. They are HTML documents with the minimum Ruby necessary to display data. If a view has conditionals that compute derived state or branch on business rules — that logic belongs elsewhere. Simple formatting methods (`truncate`, `humanize`, `titleize`, `time_ago_in_words`) are fine inline.

Push domain predicates to models (`article.edited?`, `article.byline`), reusable formatting to helpers, and reusable markup to partials. Worked good/bad example: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Keep Logic Out of Templates.

**User-facing static copy** — use **`t()`** / **`I18n.t`** and keys under **`config/locales`**, not raw strings scattered through ERB. Lazy lookup, interpolation, and key structure: **`agent_harness_rails/rules/i18n.mdc`**, **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-i18n/references/patterns.md`**.

## Reuse What the App Already Defines

Before authoring a partial, a helper, a component, or a CSS class, grep the app
for the one it already has. **Use the existing one even when it is not quite
what you would have written** — a near-miss you render, call, or apply beats a
second version a shade away from it. Two things doing one job means each later
change lands in one of them, and the app's markup drifts further apart with
every change.

Search by the name the thing would carry (§ Naming Conventions): the partial
under `app/views` (including `shared/`), the method in the domain's helper
module, the class in the app's stylesheet.

Where the existing one is close but not sufficient, **widen it** — a local with
a default, an argument to the helper, a conditional utility through
`class_names` — rather than copy it under a new name. Rewriting or restyling
something already in use changes every page that renders it, so it is a
decision to raise, not a step inside the task that noticed it.

## Action Template Skeleton

Organise every action template like this:

```erb
<%# app/views/articles/show.html.erb %>
<% provide(:title, @article.title) %>

<article id="<%= dom_id(@article) %>" class="mx-auto max-w-2xl py-8">
  <header>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900"><%= @article.title %></h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500"><%= @article.byline %></p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose mt-6">
    <%= @article.body %>
  </div>

  <section id="comments" class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Comments</h2>
    <%= render @article.comments %>
    <%= render "comments/form", article: @article, comment: @comment %>
  </section>
</article>
```

Key principles:
- Set page metadata via `provide` (single value) or `content_for` (block) at the top
- Use `dom_id` for element IDs — never hand-roll them
- Style with Tailwind utilities directly in the markup
- Delegate sub-sections to partials
- Keep the template under ~50 lines — extract partials when it grows
- The controller builds objects (`@comment = @article.comments.build`) — views don't instantiate models

Static copy is hardcoded in this skeleton (and throughout
`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`) to keep the
examples focused — in application code, user-facing strings use `t()` with keys
under `config/locales` (see the writing-i18n skill).

## Partials with Strict Locals

Every partial declares its interface with a `locals:` magic comment on its first line — Rails raises `ArgumentError` on unexpected or missing locals. No instance variables in partials — they receive everything through locals. Render with explicit locals (`render "articles/article", article: @article`); the short form `render @article` works when the partial matches the model name. Worked examples (defaults, `locals: (**)`, aliasing): **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Partials.

## Collection Rendering

Render collections with `render @collection` — Rails resolves the partial automatically and provides `*_counter` / `*_iteration`. Rendering an empty collection returns `nil`; the one-line fallback idiom (and its required parentheses), the explicit branch for richer empty states, and spacer templates: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Collections.

## Styling with Tailwind

Utility classes go directly in the ERB markup. The HTML *is* the component in Rails — partials provide reuse, not CSS abstractions. Use **`class_names`** for conditional utilities; helpers may return class strings when the same pattern repeats across templates.

**Pipeline and global CSS** — `tailwindcss-rails`, Tailwind **v3 vs v4** config shape, `@apply` policy, extending the theme, dark/responsive conventions: **`agent_harness_rails/rules/css-tailwind.mdc`**, **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-css-tailwind/references/patterns.md`**. Template-level patterns (partials, `class_names`, helpers, class order): **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Tailwind.

## Helpers for Presentation Logic

Helpers format data for display. They return strings and take values as arguments — no side effects, no state. Prefer the `tag` builder over string concatenation. Worked module and `tag` examples: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Helpers.

Use helpers for:
- Formatting (dates, numbers, currency, text)
- Conditional CSS class composition with `class_names`
- Small composed elements (badge with icon, link with label)
- Wrapping Rails helpers with domain defaults

### Organised by domain, split by size

Every helper module is available in every view — the module boundary exists for
the human reader, so draw it where the app's domain draws it:

- **One module per domain** (`ArticlesHelper`, `BillingHelper`) — not one per
  controller by reflex, and not a grab-bag.
- **`ApplicationHelper` holds only the genuinely app-wide** — nav state, page
  titles, flash rendering. A method that names a domain belongs in that
  domain's module.
- **Split by size, into a more focused domain.** Past ~100 lines, extract the
  cohesive cluster into its own module — `BillingHelper` spawns
  `BillingInvoicesHelper` — the same instinct as splitting a fat controller.
  The shared prefix keeps the domain greppable.

## Stimulus and Turbo (Hotwire)

Stimulus controllers live in **`app/javascript/controllers/`** — `data-controller`, `data-action`, targets — not inline `<script>` tags. Importmap vs bundling, entrypoints, and not fighting the pipeline: **`agent_harness_rails/rules/javascript.mdc`**, **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-javascript/references/patterns.md`**. Turbo Frames wrap regions that update in isolation; stream templates use **`action.turbo_stream.erb`**. Full patterns, morphing, broadcasts, and accidental-SPA anti-patterns: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-hotwire/references/patterns.md`**. Rule: **`agent_harness_rails/rules/hotwire.mdc`**.

## Links and Buttons

`link_to` for navigation (GET). `button_to` for mutations (POST/PATCH/DELETE) — it generates a real form, works with Turbo, and takes `data: { turbo_confirm: "..." }` for destructive actions. Worked examples: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Links and Buttons.

## Layouts and Content Blocks

Use `content_for` / `yield` to inject page-specific content into layouts. Use `provide` when setting a single value (`<% provide(:title, @article.title) %>`), `content_for` when building up content with blocks. Layout skeleton and its gotchas (`content_for(:title)` fallback, `"data-turbo-track"` quoting, nested layouts): **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Layouts.

## Form Conventions

Use `form_with` bound to a model. It infers the URL, method, and Turbo behaviour automatically. Show validation errors inline, close to the field — not as a detached error summary. Worked form, error, and upload examples: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-views/references/patterns.md`** § Forms.

**Inference is safe only while the record's state is.** `form_with model:` picks `POST` or `PATCH` from `record.persisted?`. That is the right default when the action that rendered the template fixes the state — `new` always has a new record, `edit` always a persisted one. It stops being safe the moment the same template can render both: a shared `_form` reached from several actions, a singular resource, a controller whose finder is `find_or_initialize_by`, a 422 re-render on a create that may have loaded an existing row. State the target instead of guessing at it:

```erb
<%# Bad — the verb depends on which branch produced the record %>
<%= form_with model: closure do |f| %>

<%# Good — the form declares the route it submits to %>
<%= form_with model: closure, url: card_closure_path(card), method: :post do |f| %>
```

Rule of thumb: run `bin/rails routes -g <resource>`; if the routes do not serve **both** `POST` and `PATCH`, the form must not be left to infer which one it sends. A form that emits a verb the route does not serve fails at submit time, not at render time, so nothing catches it but a spec that submits (**`agent_harness_rails/rules/testing.mdc`**).

## Decision Framework

**"Where does this logic belong?"**
- Formatting a value for display → helper method
- Simple string formatting (`.truncate`, `.humanize`, `time_ago_in_words`) → fine inline in ERB
- Deciding *what* to show (permission, state) → model predicate, controller sets the data
- Computed display value (`byline`, `summary`) → model method
- Reusable HTML fragment → partial
- Page-specific HTML → inline in the action template
- Shared page chrome → layout

**"Should this be a partial?"**
- One already exists for this content → render **that** one; don't write a second
- Used in more than one place → yes, extract
- Represents a single domain concept (a comment, a card, a form) → yes, extract
- Template exceeds ~50 lines → extract sub-sections
- Needs to be a Turbo Frame target → yes, extract into its own partial
- Needs rendering as part of a collection → yes, extract
- Reusing a styled block (same markup + Tailwind classes) → extract partial, not `@apply`
- One-off structural HTML, only a few lines → keep inline
- Would need 10+ locals → the abstraction is wrong; don't extract
- Just hiding code to make a template "look shorter" → don't extract

**"Helper or model method?"**
- Domain question (is this published? can this user edit?) → model method
- Display formatting (badge, formatted date, reading time) → helper
- Conditional CSS class composition → helper with `class_names`
- Multi-line HTML with structure (sections, lists, forms) → partial
- Small composed elements (badge with icon, link with label) → helper with `tag` builder is fine

**"How should this be styled?"** — the smallest-change ladder (utilities →
partial → helper class string → theme extension → `@apply` last resort):
**`agent_harness_rails/rules/css-tailwind.mdc`** § Utility-First. Conditional,
state-dependent appearance → `class_names`.

**"link_to or button_to?"**
- Navigating to a page (GET) → `link_to`
- Performing an action (POST/PATCH/DELETE) → `button_to`
- Destructive action → `button_to` with `data: { turbo_confirm: "..." }`

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Instead |
|-------------|---------|
| Instance variables in partials | Strict locals (`locals:` magic comment) |
| Business logic / queries in views | Controller sets instance variables, model owns predicates |
| Instantiating models in views (`Comment.new`) | Controller builds the object (`@comment = @article.comments.build`) |
| `if/else` trees for rendering variants | Separate partials or polymorphic rendering |
| Formatting in the template (`.strftime`, complex conditionals) | Helper method (but `.truncate`, `.humanize` are fine inline) |
| Inline `<script>` or `<style>` in templates | Stimulus controllers, Tailwind utilities |
| `link_to` for mutations (DELETE, POST) | `button_to` with `method:` |
| `form_with model:` where the record's persistence varies by branch | Pin `url:` and `method:` to the route that exists |
| Hand-rolled element IDs (`id="article-42"`) | `dom_id(@article)` |
| `@apply` for every styled block | Extract a partial — partials are your components |
| Arbitrary Tailwind values everywhere (`bg-[#1a2b3c]`) | Extend the Tailwind config with project tokens |
| Hardcoded CSS class strings repeated across templates | Helper returning class string, or extract partial |
| A second partial, helper, or CSS class for something the app already defines — because the existing one is slightly different | Render, call, or apply the existing one; widen it with a local, an argument, or `class_names` (§ Reuse What the App Already Defines) |
| Rendering partials without locals declaration | Add `<%# locals: (...) %>` magic comment |
| HTML string concatenation in helpers | `tag` builder or partials |
| Deep nesting of `render` calls (3+ levels) | Flatten — max 2 levels of nesting |
| `content_for` used for control flow | `content_for` / `provide` is for content injection into layouts |
| Fat helpers with many lines of HTML | Extract to a partial instead |
| Domain methods accumulating in `ApplicationHelper`, or one helper module mixing domains | Organise by domain; past ~100 lines, split the cohesive cluster into a more focused domain module (§ Organised by domain) |
| `Current.user` checks inside partials | Pass permission as boolean local, or use model predicate at the template level |

## Naming Conventions

| Thing | Convention | Examples |
|-------|-----------|----------|
| Action templates | `action.html.erb` | `show.html.erb`, `index.html.erb` |
| Partials | `_noun.html.erb` (singular) | `_article.html.erb`, `_comment.html.erb` |
| Form partials | `_form.html.erb` | `_form.html.erb` |
| Shared partials | `app/views/shared/` or `app/views/application/` | `shared/_flash.html.erb` |
| Helper modules | Named for the app domain; oversized modules split into a more focused domain, keeping the prefix | `ArticlesHelper`, `BillingHelper` → `BillingInvoicesHelper` |
| Helper methods | Verb or noun describing the output | `reading_time`, `status_badge`, `btn_classes` |
| Layouts | Purpose-named | `application.html.erb`, `admin.html.erb`, `mailer.html.erb` |
| Turbo stream templates | `action.turbo_stream.erb` | `create.turbo_stream.erb` |
| Spacer templates | `_spacer.html.erb` | `_spacer.html.erb` |

## Verification

Before finishing, verify:

- [ ] No instance variables in partials — everything comes through strict locals
- [ ] Every partial has a `<%# locals: (...) %>` magic comment
- [ ] No queries, business logic, or complex conditionals in templates
- [ ] No model instantiation in views — controller builds objects
- [ ] Presentation logic lives in helpers, not inline in ERB
- [ ] Element IDs use `dom_id`, not hand-rolled strings
- [ ] Collections rendered with `render @collection`, not manual loops
- [ ] Forms use `form_with model:`, not `form_tag` or `form_for`
- [ ] Any form whose record may be new **or** persisted pins `url:` and `method:` — inference only holds while the record's state is fixed (`bin/rails routes -g <resource>` confirms the verb exists)
- [ ] Mutations use `button_to`, not `link_to` with `turbo_method`
- [ ] Destructive actions have `data: { turbo_confirm: "..." }`
- [ ] Templates are under ~50 lines — sub-sections extracted to partials
- [ ] Turbo Frames wrap the right granularity of content
- [ ] Page titles and meta set via `provide` / `content_for`, not hardcoded in layout
- [ ] Helpers return strings and have no side effects
- [ ] Helper modules are organised by app domain — `ApplicationHelper` only for the genuinely app-wide, and any module past ~100 lines split into a more focused domain helper
- [ ] Nothing you added duplicates a partial, helper, or CSS class the app already defines — you grepped by name before authoring it
- [ ] Tailwind utilities used directly in markup — partials for reuse, not `@apply`
- [ ] Conditional classes use `class_names` helper
- [ ] Stimulus attributes use `data-controller`, `data-action`, `data-[name]-target`
- [ ] No inline `<script>` or `<style>` tags
